@startuml
title AISafe Flight Management System\nProposed Distributed Architecture (4 services)

left to right direction
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam componentStyle rectangle
skinparam packageStyle rectangle
skinparam defaultTextAlignment center

actor "Client / Frontend" as Client

package "Aircraft Management" as AircraftPkg {
  component "Aircraft Service\n\nMain concepts owned:\n- AircraftModel\n- Aircraft\n- AircraftAvailability\n- AircraftCertification\n\n2+ replicas" as Aircraft
  database "Independent local store\nper replica" as AircraftDB
  Aircraft --> AircraftDB
}

package "Airport Management" as AirportPkg {
  component "Airport Service\n\nMain concepts owned:\n- Airport\n- Runway\n- Facilities\n- Certification\n- AirportStatus\n\n2+ replicas" as Airport
  database "Independent local store\nper replica" as AirportDB
  Airport --> AirportDB
}

package "Route / Flight Operations" as RoutePkg {
  component "Route / Flight Ops Service\n\nMain concepts owned:\n- Route\n- RouteHistory\n- Flight\n\n2+ replicas" as Route
  database "Independent local store\nper replica" as RouteDB
  Route --> RouteDB
}

package "Maintenance Management" as MaintenancePkg {
  component "Maintenance Service\n\nMain concepts owned:\n- MaintenanceTemplate\n- MaintenanceRecord\n\n2+ replicas" as Maintenance
  database "Independent local store\nper replica" as MaintenanceDB
  Maintenance --> MaintenanceDB
}

Client --> Aircraft : HTTP/REST
Client --> Airport : HTTP/REST
Client --> Route : HTTP/REST
Client --> Maintenance : HTTP/REST

Route --> Aircraft : HTTP/REST\naircraft lookup, status,\nrange and capacity;\nupdate flight metrics
Route --> Airport : HTTP/REST\nairport lookup, status\nand airport certifications
Route --> Maintenance : HTTP/REST\nmaintenance status
Maintenance --> Aircraft : HTTP/REST\naircraft lookup,\nmodel and flight hours

Aircraft --> Route : HTTP/REST\ncompatible routes,\nflight queries and statistics
Airport --> Route : HTTP/REST\nairport routes\nand route statistics

rectangle "Replica collaboration pattern\n(applies inside every service)" as Replication {
  component "Replica A" as RA
  component "Replica B" as RB
  database "Local Store A" as RDBA
  database "Local Store B" as RDBB

  RA <--> RB : peer-to-peer\nHTTP GET forwarding
  RA --> RDBA
  RB --> RDBB
}

note bottom of Replication
Each replica first checks its local data.
If the required data is missing or incomplete,
it queries peers and returns a coherent response.
Distributed collections may require aggregation
of local and peer results, with duplicate handling.

Forwarding must handle timeouts and unavailable
peers, and prevent circular forwarding.

GET forwarding retrieves data from peers.
It does not, by itself, synchronize writes
or guarantee eventual consistency.

Consistency target: eventual consistency.
Write synchronization, conflict resolution and
data redundancy must be defined separately.
end note

note bottom of Route
Route / Flight Operations owns routes and flights
and coordinates cross-domain checks.
Flight is the existing PSOFT entity used
to represent scheduled flight operations.

References to data owned by other services
must use identifiers and HTTP/REST APIs,
instead of cross-service JPA relationships
or direct repository/database access.
end note

note as ArchitecturalRules
Architectural rules:
1. Clear business-domain ownership.
2. No shared database between services.
3. Independent local data store per replica.
4. Inter-service communication via HTTP/REST.
5. At least two replicas per service.
6. Peer-to-peer GET forwarding and response aggregation.
7. Inter-service authentication, HTTPS/TLS, RBAC and audit logging.
8. No event-based communication or message queues in Assignment 1.

The stores inside the service packages summarize
the per-replica storage shown in the replica pattern.
The arrows describe the proposed distributed APIs,
including queries needed to preserve current endpoints.
They do not imply that these APIs are already implemented.
end note

note as DDDExplanation
DDD - Domain-Driven Design

The proposed architecture groups related business responsibilities
into four Bounded Contexts with explicit data ownership.

- Aircraft Management:
  Owns aircraft models, aircraft instances, aircraft availability
  and aircraft certifications.

- Airport Management:
  Owns airports, runways, facilities, airport status
  and airport certifications (Certification).

- Route / Flight Operations:
  Owns routes, route history and flights.

- Maintenance Management:
  Owns maintenance templates and maintenance records.

AircraftCertification and Certification are distinct PSOFT classes.
The current airport Certification entity does not identify an aircraft model;
model-to-airport eligibility must be aligned with the original requirements.

Each context owns its business logic and data.
Cross-context interactions use well-defined HTTP/REST APIs.
The concept lists show the main concepts, not every class.

The four-service division is based on business responsibilities,
data ownership and clear domain boundaries.
Having four team members supports this organization,
but was not the main criterion for defining the services.
end note

@enduml